    "evaluation_of_current_stalemate": "The debate has reached peak entrenchment. Both camps have laid out their complete intellectual artillery. Camp B has heavily fortified around: (1) The minimal pair (Choice D has sgRNA7, Choice B removes it), and (2) The screening forward-selection logic (Colleague 8's new point: positive activators would have dropped out of the screen, so top hits must be putative repressors whose failure in vivo proves they don't act as repressors/activators).",
    "vulnerabilities_in_B_camp_to_exploit": [
      "Colleague 8 made a subtle but critical assertion: that in the primary screen, 'cells were sorted for those able to proliferate upon growth factor activation... If a gene were an essential positive factor, cells would fail to proliferate and be lost.' BUT LOOK AT WHAT THE SCREEN SAYS: 'Next, the quiescent cells were transformed with a lentiviral vector carrying Cas9 enzyme and a library of gsRNAs targeting 23,000 genes. Five days after transduction the quiescent cells were activated with growth factors and after 4 and 14 days... sorted the cells that were able to proliferate.' In a whole-genome knockout screen targeting 23,000 genes, thousands of cells proliferate under growth factors! The top 10 hits were simply sgRNAs found in proliferating cells. Without a non-activated control or depletion sequencing comparison in the prompt, top hits in a proliferation sort can include passenger guides, growth-factor hyper-sensitizers, OR false positives. More importantly: Experiment 1 in vivo did NOT give growth factors!",
      "Camp B's central psychometric claim is that 'an author would never write a minimal pair if H was the key.' This can be directly dismantled: what if this question was originally written with B as key in an imperfect draft, or what if the question comes from an advanced olympiad (IBO/USABO) where 'None of the above' is notoriously used precisely when all positive claims are biologically invalid?",
